00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:48.530
a definition that I'm going to
be operationalizing or using, in

00:10:48.530 --> 00:10:53.720
my research of what colonialism
is. So, in particular, what I'm

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:57.740
talking about his physical
colonialism. And what I mean by

00:10:57.740 --> 00:11:01.850
that are the movement of people
from what are called source

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:06.080
communities, or metropolitan
communities into, which are

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:09.140
where they come from, into these
target bases, or target

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:13.100
communities, which are foreign
places, and also movements of

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:16.010
people where they're moving into
these spaces, and they they are

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:19.040
intending to permanently reside
in those spaces. So there's a

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:24.320
lot of there was a lot of
rhetorical back and forth and

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:27.440
sort of arguing about
colonialism as versus

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:31.640
imperialism. So a lot of the
discussions revolve around the

00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:36.050
permanency of a vs versus
imperialism, which tends to be

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:39.920
less permanent, and is sort of a
shuffling of bureaucratic and

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:42.650
military folks into a space and
then shuffling them out. Again.

00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:47.450
Another important feature of
colonialism, which

00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:51.440
differentiates it from other
kinds of movements of people, is

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:53.930
that there is a notion where
that the people that are going

00:11:53.930 --> 00:11:58.400
to these foreign spaces,
maintain political identity and

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:01.910
rely on the original state for
their identity, their

00:12:01.910 --> 00:12:05.810
protection, the resources, they
need to sort of maintain their

00:12:05.810 --> 00:12:09.710
colonial presence in this target
communities. So these are some

00:12:09.710 --> 00:12:13.310
very basic definitional
qualities that I'm using when

00:12:13.310 --> 00:12:16.190
I'm when I'm talking about what
a colony is, and what

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:20.060
colonialism is. One of the
things that's also important in

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:22.700
this project, and this isn't
something that I've found in my

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:26.540
research on defining colonialism
are talking about what it means

00:12:26.840 --> 00:12:31.070
is this this idea of stages of
colonialism. So by reading a lot

00:12:31.070 --> 00:12:35.450
of colonial tracks, it emerged
for me that there are different

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:39.170
stages of colonialism. The first
stage is what I call the

00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:42.380
exploration stage, which is
essentially you send out

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:49.910
people or in the case of Mars
instruments that are there to

00:12:49.910 --> 00:12:53.120
sort of understand that the
resources and the conditions of

00:12:53.570 --> 00:12:58.190
the space that is, and to decide
where it has potential for

00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:01.700
colonization, then you have the
planting stage, which is where

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:05.720
you put small outposts there. If
you think back to the early

00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:08.900
colonial period in North
America, these are typically

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:13.490
forts with military folks that
were there to establish a

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:17.780
foothold. But they weren't sort
of full blown colonies. And

00:13:17.780 --> 00:13:20.090
next, of course, you have the
settlement phase, which is where

00:13:21.080 --> 00:13:26.750
these forts begin to expand, and
we get different sorts of people

00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:31.580
that begin to live there. So
children, women, people from

00:13:31.580 --> 00:13:35.990
different walks of life, like,
you know, blacksmiths, and and,

00:13:36.050 --> 00:13:39.320
and barrel makers, and so forth.
Right. So it's not just sort of

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:44.150
a military operation, but it's a
diversified, more heterogeneous

00:13:44.150 --> 00:13:48.140
population. And finally, the
very last stage of colonialism

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:51.530
is of course, when it
disappears. So it either

00:13:51.770 --> 00:13:55.760
disappears to emancipation. So
in the United States, obviously,

00:13:55.760 --> 00:13:59.150
after the after the war for
independence, the United States

00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:02.780
became its own countries that
was emancipated from its

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:05.990
original colonial source, or
it's Incorporated. So you can

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:08.480
think about Ireland and
Scotland, for example, which

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:12.140
became part of, of Britain in
the United Kingdom. And so

00:14:12.140 --> 00:14:15.110
therefore, there's a sort of
like two ways they can go, you

00:14:15.110 --> 00:14:18.170
can either emancipate or you can
become Incorporated, and you

00:14:18.170 --> 00:14:23.450
lose your status as a colony.
For this particular project, I

00:14:23.450 --> 00:14:27.770
looked at two sets of texts. So
