survival possible. So I'm off
azimoff writes, for example, we

00:31:30.810 --> 00:31:34.410
can we can do it and earth men
can't. They've got a real world,

00:31:34.410 --> 00:31:37.740
they've got open sky and food,
getting into a ship will

00:31:37.740 --> 00:31:40.590
challenge them, right. We've
been living on a ship our entire

00:31:40.590 --> 00:31:44.400
lives, breathing package, their
drinking package, water, we eat

00:31:44.400 --> 00:31:47.910
the same food and rations, we
eat aboard a ship, we get into a

00:31:47.910 --> 00:31:51.000
ship the same thing we've known
all our lives. So the colonial

00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:56.250
conditions themselves, condition
that settlers to actually be

00:31:56.250 --> 00:32:01.200
able to survive in ways that
folks that are not Martian can't

00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:04.740
do so they can sort of solve
problems in ways that Earth

00:32:04.740 --> 00:32:06.120
people can solve problems.

00:32:07.650 --> 00:32:09.750
So now I'm going to switch a
little bit and talk about

00:32:09.750 --> 00:32:14.850
inspirational rhetoric. And I
call this this argument that of

00:32:14.850 --> 00:32:18.300
inspirational rhetoric as an
argument for larger arguments

00:32:18.300 --> 00:32:21.360
through adversity. And what we
see here is what's called a

00:32:21.360 --> 00:32:27.330
means ends argument. So in this
kind of argument, the the the

00:32:27.330 --> 00:32:30.390
person that the colonist is
submitting themselves or the

00:32:30.390 --> 00:32:32.730
potential columnist, is
submitting themselves to

00:32:32.730 --> 00:32:36.870
extraordinary trials, which are
the means. And the ends are the

00:32:36.870 --> 00:32:40.050
extraordinary benefits that you
reap through these trials. So

00:32:40.050 --> 00:32:43.380
the idea is that going to Mars
is hard, but through this

00:32:43.380 --> 00:32:48.870
hardship, we get these unique
benefits. And there is a there's

00:32:48.870 --> 00:32:52.950
a rich history of this kind of
argument in our own colonial

00:32:53.280 --> 00:32:57.540
experience and our own colonial
tracts. So Frederick tracts and

00:32:57.540 --> 00:33:02.430
Turner on in his very famous
piece on the frontier, right? It

00:33:02.430 --> 00:33:05.730
is to the frontier. The American
intellect owes its striking

00:33:05.730 --> 00:33:09.000
characteristics, that coarseness
of strength combined with

00:33:09.000 --> 00:33:12.570
acuteness, acquisitive,
inquisitiveness and practical

00:33:12.750 --> 00:33:16.260
that practical, inventive turn
of the mind, quick to find

00:33:16.260 --> 00:33:21.180
expedience, and masterful grabs,
grasp of material things are

00:33:21.180 --> 00:33:23.760
traits are called out elsewhere
because of the existence of the

00:33:23.760 --> 00:33:28.410
frontier. So the notion is that
the frontier, in some ways, is

00:33:28.410 --> 00:33:31.770
responsible for the kind of
traits that made America America

00:33:32.010 --> 00:33:36.390
that made it great that made it
unique. And so we, but it's only

00:33:36.390 --> 00:33:38.550
through the trials of the
frontier, that you get those

00:33:38.550 --> 00:33:41.700
kind of qualities. And we see
this very same argument being

00:33:41.700 --> 00:33:46.320
made in science fiction texts. A
perfect example of this is

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:50.100
Arthur C. Clarke, the red sands
of Mars. And in this book, we

00:33:50.100 --> 00:33:54.720
have the protagonist, Martin
Gibson as a journalist. And he's

00:33:54.720 --> 00:33:57.180
sort of writing an expo, say on
Mars, he's going to church

00:33:57.300 --> 00:33:59.940
Journey to Mars and talk about
what colonial life is like and

00:33:59.940 --> 00:34:03.600
so forth. And he's really
skeptical, first about, about

00:34:03.600 --> 00:34:07.140
the colonial project, right? He
says, you know, look, from the

00:34:07.140 --> 00:34:10.170
point of view of Earth, you
know, Mars is a long way, it

00:34:10.170 --> 00:34:13.740
costs a lot of money, it doesn't
offer anything, at the question

00:34:13.740 --> 00:34:16.560
that we're all asking is, what
do we get out of it? Right? So

00:34:16.590 --> 00:34:19.410
the whole point of this novel,
then is to answer this question,

00:34:19.410 --> 00:34:22.170
what do we get out of it? And
after spending time in the

00:34:22.170 --> 00:34:27.030
colony, Gibson has this sort of
a pivotal experience. And then

00:34:27.030 --> 00:34:30.420
he becomes a strong advocate. In
fact, he becomes the PR person

00:34:30.420 --> 00:34:36.480
from Mars. And what he finds is
that he experiences when he gets

00:34:36.480 --> 00:34:42.060
in with the folks on Mars right.
He begins to understand that

00:34:42.060 --> 00:34:44.640
what they gain from being on
Mars in their colonial

00:34:44.640 --> 00:34:48.900
experience, right, is this
keen-eyed competence and

00:34:48.900 --> 00:34:52.620
readiness to take well
calculated risks, which enabled

00:34:52.620 --> 00:34:55.290
them not merely to survive on
this heartbreakingly hostile

00:34:55.290 --> 00:34:58.590
world world, but to lay the
foundations the first

00:34:58.590 --> 00:35:02.400
extraterrestrial culture So this
notion that, you know, the

00:35:02.400 --> 00:35:06.810
utopia or a return to utopia
begins to develop through

00:35:07.050 --> 00:35:11.640
existing within the harsh
conditions of Mars. Another

00:35:11.760 --> 00:35:16.380
example of utopian rhetoric is
in Kim Stanley Robinson's novel

00:35:16.380 --> 00:35:21.030
read Mars, which is part of a
trilogy. So what's interesting

00:35:21.030 --> 00:35:25.050
is that in in Robinson's novel,
there are 100 colonists that are

00:35:25.050 --> 00:35:28.740
the original colonists and Mars.
And the whole point of this

00:35:28.740 --> 00:35:32.850
novel is to talk about how they
struggle to realize their

00:35:32.850 --> 00:35:37.080
utopian vision for a particular
colony. And it's really the

00:35:37.080 --> 00:35:42.570
struggle itself, and sort of
maintaining a pure space for

00:35:42.570 --> 00:35:47.970
Mars, which is, which is the
struggle that if they succeed,

00:35:47.970 --> 00:35:51.420
will have this sort of utopian
benefits. So one of the main

00:35:51.420 --> 00:35:54.840
characters writes, all of our
says, all of our governments are

00:35:54.840 --> 00:35:58.470
flawed. It's why history is such
a bloody mess. We are now on our

00:35:58.470 --> 00:36:01.650
own and I, for one, have no
intention of repeating mistakes.

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:04.170
We are the first Martian
colonists, we are the

00:36:04.170 --> 00:36:07.440
scientists, it is our job to
think things new, and make them

00:36:07.440 --> 00:36:11.580
new. So the idea is that Mars is
really a tabula rasa, in which

00:36:11.580 --> 00:36:15.630
we can build this ideal society
on and the struggle is not so

00:36:15.630 --> 00:36:18.450
much the struggle of existence
on Mars, but the struggle to

00:36:18.450 --> 00:36:23.250
keep this a free space in which
to create this ideal colony or

00:36:23.250 --> 00:36:25.800
this ideal utopia that the
Martians want.

00:36:27.060 --> 00:36:30.270
So now very briefly, I'm just
going to talk about the the sort

00:36:30.270 --> 00:36:34.800
of the the contrast we see
between the rhetoric of early

00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:38.280
modern colonialism, the rhetoric
of science fiction, and Martian

00:36:38.280 --> 00:36:42.120
colonialism. So early modern
promotional rhetoric is devoted

00:36:42.120 --> 00:36:45.510
to reassuring potential
colonists and supporters by

00:36:45.510 --> 00:36:49.080
dismissing and diminishing the
risks of colonial life, right?

00:36:49.110 --> 00:36:50.730
Oh, don't worry about the
serpent's Don't worry about

00:36:50.730 --> 00:36:54.720
